
<sect>Error Processing (Revision 1.2 -- 04/05/98)

<p> On many unix systems, simple commands report 
their error messages to standard error;
this normally is the screen of the calling terminal.
To keep the messages of different processes apart, each
process usually prints its own name and a verbose error
description.

<p>To facilitate the programming of such error messages, <tt/errproc.h/
provides some variables containing the process' name.
To initialize those variables, the function <tt/errinit()/
needs to be called at program startup with <tt/argv[0]/ as
argument.

<p>These extensions make it possible to write self-contained error-reporting
subroutines that require no explicit cooperation from <tt/main/.

<p>
<tscreen><verb>
 char * program_invocation_name
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This variable's value is the name that was used to invoke the
program running in the current process. It is the same as <tt/argv[0]/
Note that this is not necessarily a useful file name; often it contains
no directory names.

<p>
<tscreen><verb>
 char * program_invocation_short_name
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This variable's value is the name that was used to invoke the program
running in the current process, with directory names removed. (That is
to say, it is the same as <tt/program_invocation_name/ minus everything
up to the last slash, if any.)

These two variables are set up with the following function:

<sect1>errinit
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
 void errinit {const char *program_name}
</verb></tscreen>

<p>This function initializes the two variables described above using the string
that gets passed to it by <tt/program_name/. It is intended to be called as the 
first function within <tt/main/ passing <tt/argv[0]/ as argument.

<p>These extensions make it possible to write self-contained error-reporting
subroutines that require no explicit cooperation from <tt/main/.

<p> Here is an example on how to use the features described above in your program:

<tscreen><verb>
/*
 *  print an error message
 */
if (error == TRUE)
   fprintf (stderr, "%s : Error in routine 'important'\n",
            program_invocation_short_name);
</verb></tscreen>

<p>

<sect1>puterr resp. gputerr
<p>
To write an error message to the standard error, to a logfile or to the syslog facility, use the following functions:
<tscreen><verb>
void puterr { const char *gglpart, const char *routine)

void gputerr { const char *gglpart, const char *routine)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>To the variable <tt/gglpart/, you can hand over the library object,
to the variable <tt/routine/, you can hand over the funcion
name. Unterschied MS  

